| Thread                                                                   | Runnable                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| It is a **class**.                                                       | It is an **interface**.                                         |
| We use `extends Thread`.                                                 | We use `implements Runnable`.                                   |
| We cannot extend another class because Java supports single inheritance. | We can extend another class because `Runnable` is an interface. |
| Thread and task are in the **same class**.                               | Task and Thread are **separate**.                               |
| Less flexible.                                                           | More flexible and generally preferred.                          |



-> What is Thread class?
Ans : Thread is a class in Java used to create and run a thread.

                   Thread Class
                         |
        ┌────────────────┼─────────────────┐
        ↓                ↓                 ↓
   Thread Start       Thread Control    Thread Info
        |                |                 |
        ,   
        
        
     start()           sleep()           getName()
     run()             join()            setName()
                       interrupt()       getId()
                       yield()           isAlive()
                       wait()*
                       notify()*`
                       notifyAll()*
                       
                       
                       
                       | Method        | Simple English Meaning                              
| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| `start()`     | Starts a new thread                                 |
| `run()`       | Contains the task/code executed by the thread       |
| `sleep()`     | Pauses the thread for a specific amount of time     |
| `join()`      | Makes one thread wait until another thread finishes |
| `yield()`     | Gives other threads a chance to execute             |
| `interrupt()` | Interrupts a thread                                 |
| `getName()`   | Returns the thread's name                           |
| `setName()`   | Changes the thread's name                           |
| `isAlive()`   | Checks whether the thread is still running          |
| `getId()`     | Returns the thread's ID                             |
                       
                       
                       | Method        | Simple meaning                                                
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `start()`     | New thread start karta hai                                    |
| `run()`       | Thread ka task/code                                           |
| `sleep()`     | Thread ko kuch time ke liye sleep karta hai                   |
| `join()`      | Ek thread ko doosre thread ke finish hone tak wait karata hai |
| `yield()`     | Current thread doosre threads ko chance deta hai              |
| `interrupt()` | Thread ko interrupt karta hai                                 |
| `getName()`   | Thread ka name deta hai                                       |
| `setName()`   | Thread ka name change karta hai                               |
| `isAlive()`   | Check karta hai thread running hai ya nahi                    |
| `getId()`     | Thread ki ID deta hai                                         |
          
 - Note: wait(), notify(), notifyAll() technically Thread class ke methods nahi, Object class ke methods hain.
   Threading mein use hote hain, isliye aksar saath mein discuss kiye jaate hain.     
   
   
->  What is Runnable interface?
Ans : Runnable is an interface in Java used to define the task that a thread will execute.

       Runnable Interface
              |
              ↓
        ┌─────────────┐
        │   run()     │
        └─────────────┘    

Q:1  Runnable interface has one abstract method: run().
Ans : Meaning: run() ke andar hum thread ka task/code likhte hain.

-> Runnable's run() method has a void return type, so it cannot return a result.
-> Callable's call() method can return a result and can throw checked exceptions.

----------------------🔥 Java Multithreading Roadmap------------------

1. Thread Basics ⭐⭐⭐
What is a Thread?
Process vs Thread
Multitasking vs Multithreading
Advantages of multithreading
Main thread
Thread name

2. Creating Threads ⭐⭐⭐
Extending Thread
Implementing Runnable
Callable
Future
FutureTask
Thread
 ├── Thread class
 ├── Runnable
 └── Callable
 
3. Thread Methods ⭐⭐⭐

You should know:

start()
run()
sleep()
join()
yield()
interrupt()
isAlive()
currentThread()
setName()
getName()

4. Thread Lifecycle ⭐⭐⭐
NEW
 ↓
RUNNABLE
 ↓
RUNNING
 ↓
BLOCKED / WAITING / TIMED_WAITING
 ↓
TERMINATED

Important difference between:

BLOCKED
WAITING
TIMED_WAITING

5. Race Condition ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

When multiple threads access and modify the same shared data and the final result becomes incorrect/unpredictable.

count++;

Two threads doing this simultaneously can cause problems.

6. Synchronization ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
synchronized
synchronized method
synchronized block
object lock
class lock
intrinsic monitor

Example:

synchronized void increment() {
    count++;
}
7. Lock ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Very important for backend interviews.

Learn:

Lock
ReentrantLock
lock()
unlock()
tryLock()
lockInterruptibly()

Example:

Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

lock.lock();

try {
    count++;
} finally {
    lock.unlock();
}

8. volatile ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Used for visibility between threads.

volatile boolean running = true;
volatile in Java ⭐⭐⭐⭐

-> volatile is used in multithreading to make sure that when one thread changes a variable
 other threads can see the latest value.

Important:

volatile provides visibility, but it does not make compound operations like count++ atomic.

9. Atomic Classes ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Learn:

AtomicInteger
AtomicLong
AtomicBoolean

Example:

AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

count.incrementAndGet();

Useful when you need atomic operations without using synchronized for simple cases.

10. Inter-Thread Communication ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Very important.

Learn:

wait()
notify()
notifyAll()

Example:

Producer
    ↓
 Shared Resource
    ↓
Consumer

Remember:

wait() releases the object's monitor/lock and waits.

11. Producer–Consumer ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Modern approach:

BlockingQueue<Integer> queue =
        new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(5);

Producer:

queue.put(10);

Consumer:

int data = queue.take();

Also learn:

BlockingQueue
ArrayBlockingQueue
LinkedBlockingQueue
12. Executor Framework ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Very important for real backend applications.

Learn:

Executor
ExecutorService
Executors
submit()
execute()
shutdown()
shutdownNow()

Example:

ExecutorService service =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

service.submit(() -> {
    System.out.println("Task running");
});

service.shutdown();
13. Thread Pool ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Instead of creating a new thread for every request:

1000 requests
      ↓
Thread Pool
      ↓
Reusable Threads

Learn:

Fixed Thread Pool
Cached Thread Pool
Scheduled Thread Pool
Single Thread Executor

And importantly:

Why thread pools are better than creating unlimited threads.

14. Callable & Future ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Runnable doesn't return a result.

Callable can return a result.

Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    return 100;
};

Then:

Future<Integer> future = executor.submit(task);

Integer result = future.get();
15. Concurrent Collections ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Very important for interviews.

Learn:

ConcurrentHashMap
CopyOnWriteArrayList
BlockingQueue
ConcurrentLinkedQueue

Especially:

ConcurrentHashMap

Multiple threads can work concurrently without locking the entire map for ordinary operations.

16. Deadlock ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Two threads wait for each other's locks forever.

Thread 1
   ↓
Lock A
   ↓
waiting for Lock B

Thread 2
   ↓
Lock B
   ↓
waiting for Lock A

Result:

DEADLOCK ❌

Learn:

What is deadlock?
How to detect it?
How to prevent it?
17. Starvation ⭐⭐⭐

A thread keeps waiting because other threads continuously get the required resources.

18. Livelock ⭐⭐⭐

Threads are active but don't make useful progress.

Difference:

Deadlock → Threads are stuck/waiting

Livelock → Threads are running but no progress
19. Atomicity, Visibility & Ordering ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

These are the core concepts behind concurrency.

Atomicity

Operation happens completely or not at all.

Visibility

One thread can see another thread's updated value.

Ordering

Order in which operations become visible to other threads.

These three are very important for understanding:

synchronized
volatile
AtomicInteger
Lock
20. Java Memory Model (JMM) ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Learn the basic idea:

Thread 1
   ↓
Working Memory
   ↓
     Main Memory
   ↑
Working Memory
   ↑
Thread 2

Important terms:

Main memory
Thread working memory
Visibility
Happens-before relationship
            